# NCAA Performance Technologies
## What the new guidance means for us

Applied Performance | Staff Meeting | April 2026

---

# Why are we talking about this?

- The NCAA Sport Science Institute put out new guidance on how schools should handle performance technologies. That covers wearables, GPS units, body comp tools, wellness apps, cameras, surveys, and anything else that collects data on our athletes.

- The message is pretty simple: every school should have a written plan that spells out who owns the data, how it gets used, and how student-athletes are protected.

- We don't have one. I think we should build one before someone tells us to.

---

# The Five NCAA recommendations

| # | Area | What it means |
|---|------|---------------|
| 1 | Policy | Write the plan. Include people from across the department. Keep it current. |
| 2 | Education | Train everyone who touches the data, at least once a year. Different content for different roles. |
| 3 | Data management | Decide who owns the data, who can see it, where it lives, and what happens when an athlete transfers. |
| 4 | Tech selection | Vet tools before you buy them. No single department makes that call alone. |
| 5 | Continuous improvement | Ask student-athletes and staff how things are going. Actually use the answers. |

---

# What we're proposing

- First thing is getting the right people in a room. We'd form a working group with reps from sports medicine, S&C, coaching, compliance, legal, IT, and student-athletes. We'd also run a technology inventory across all sports so we actually know what we're working with.

- From there, we draft the written plan, put together a consent template, and build a checklist for evaluating new tech purchases. We'd bring all of that to S-A Services Staff for feedback before we finalize anything.

- Summer is when we roll out training and updated consent forms. By fall, this becomes part of how we operate: annual reviews, training built into orientation and preseason, ongoing feedback from athletes and staff.

---

# Why this matters

Picture a student-athlete who starts obsessing over a sleep score or a body comp number. The NCAA calls that out as a real risk. If we have a plan in place, we've already thought through how to handle it.

For the rest of us, it means a clear process. You'll know what data you can access, what you're expected to do with it, and who to ask when you're not sure.

This is all guidance right now, not a mandate. But I'd rather be the school that's already figured it out than the one scrambling to catch up.

---

<!-- _class: lead -->

# Questions?

Next step is forming the working group and starting the tech inventory.

If you want in on the conversation, come find me.

